## After this comes the fun part 🚀

Right now you're still starting each container manually:

```bash
docker run ...
```

The next step is **Docker Compose**.

Instead of two separate commands, you'll have a single `docker-compose.yml` at the project root, and you'll be able to start the entire stack with:

```bash
docker compose up
```

That will launch:

* 🦀 Rust backend
* ⚛️ React frontend
* (Soon) PostgreSQL
* (Later) Redis

With one command. That's much closer to how production development works, and it's exactly the kind of workflow you'll see on teams using Docker and Kubernetes.

Awesome. This is the part where your project starts to feel like a **real** full-stack application.

Your structure should now look like:

```text
toast-rust-dashboard/
│
├── backend/
│   ├── Dockerfile
│   └── .dockerignore
│
├── frontend/
│   ├── Dockerfile
│   └── .dockerignore
│
└── docker-compose.yml   <-- we're creating this
```

